CopyPasteBin split Debian packages
==================================

The `copypastebin` package is a small complete-installation package. It depends
on the exactly matching `copypastebin-backend` and
`gnome-shell-extension-copypastebin` packages. The backend can be installed on
its own for use with a separately distributed extension.

The extension package does not enable the extension during installation
because package manager actions run as root and must not modify an arbitrary
user's GNOME settings.

After installation, log out and back in to GNOME Wayland. Open the application
menu and choose "CopyPasteBin". This
explicit action enables only the CopyPasteBin extension for the currently
logged-in user. It does not enable the extension for other accounts. The
launcher refuses activation while a user-local extension with the same UUID
exists, preventing GNOME from silently selecting older code.

During that explicit per-user activation, the launcher also asks GTK to
rebuild only the current user's hicolor icon cache. This repairs a stale icon
entry that an older offline uninstaller could leave after removing its local
CopyPasteBin icon. The repair does not read CopyPasteBin settings, history or
clipboard data, and failure to refresh the cache does not block activation.

The package contains AppStream metadata, the GPL-3.0-or-later project license,
the developer name, homepage and product icon. A graphical installer may still
describe a locally downloaded DEB as untrusted or from an unknown publisher
because it does not come from a configured, signed package repository. A
native DEB is not sandboxed, so Ubuntu may also describe its permissions as
unrestricted. These system warnings are not hidden or bypassed by the package.

If CopyPasteBin was previously installed from the offline archive, run that
archive's current uninstall.sh before installing this package. The offline
uninstaller preserves ~/.config/copypastebin and ~/.local/state/copypastebin.
A user-local extension with the same UUID takes precedence over this system
extension. The system extension uses `/usr/bin/cpb` first when both backends
are available, but an older user-local copy of the extension could keep using
the old user-local backend. The DEB launcher
therefore requires removal of the old offline program code before activation;
it does not remove that code or private data automatically.

Removing any of the split packages removes only package-owned program files.
User settings and metadata history under ~/.config/copypastebin and
~/.local/state/copypastebin are not package-owned and remain in place. Removing
only the extension package leaves the backend installed.

To remove the complete split installation explicitly, remove
`copypastebin`, `gnome-shell-extension-copypastebin`, and
`copypastebin-backend`. Removing only the small `copypastebin` metapackage
does not by itself prove that the two dependency packages were removed.
